# Design and architecture

- **Canonical design**: Before implementing features, changing architecture, or adding agents/artifacts, read [docs/multi-agent-game-builder-design.md](docs/multi-agent-game-builder-design.md). It is the single source of truth for tech choices, user flows, Temporal workflow architecture, LangGraph4j team graphs, pipeline, and task breakdown.
- **Module-local onions**: There are no repo-wide top-level `domain`, `application`, `infrastructure`, or `interfaces` packages. Each backend module/bounded context owns those layers locally: e.g. `orchestration.domain`, `orchestration.application`, `team.design.domain`, `team.design.application`, etc.
- **Orchestration bounded context**: Generation workflow coordination lives under `com.cyborgstudios.gamebuilder.orchestration`. Orchestration coordinates teams but does not contain team logic.
- **Studio teams**: Team logic is organized in peer modules under `com.cyborgstudios.gamebuilder.team.<name>` (design, story, art, engineering, qa, direction). Each team owns the logic that produces its artifacts; place new agent or reflection code in the correct team-local layer, usually `team.<name>.application`. Do not add "generic" agent code outside a team.
- **DDD/onion**: In every module, domain has no dependency on application or infrastructure. Application depends only on local domain and explicit ports/contracts. New orchestration domain concepts go in `orchestration.domain.*`; new team-specific domain concepts go in `team.<name>.domain.*`; framework-neutral use cases and ports go in `orchestration.application.*`; REST and DTOs for orchestration go in `orchestration.interfaces.web`.
- **Framework boundaries**: Temporal owns durable project-level workflows. LangGraph4j is only for bounded team reasoning graphs inside activities. Spring annotations, Temporal annotations/clients, LangGraph4j, and provider SDK types must not import into domain models, application use cases/ports, REST DTOs, or persisted artifact contracts.
- **Artifacts and files**: Structured data lives as JSON in artifacts. Unstructured data (images, audio, generated assets) is stored as files/blobs; artifacts reference them via JSON (e.g. `BINARY_ASSET` with payload `{ "filePath" }` or `{ "blobId" }`; `AssetList` entries with `fileRef`). Keep schema-driven contracts; only the reference is in JSON.
- **Gaps**: If the design is silent on a detail, follow the "Handling gaps" section in the design doc: domain defaults + REFLECTION_NOTE; architectural changes must respect onion layers; new artifact types or teams require updating the design or documenting in REFLECTION_NOTE.
